60,3,1,talk,Victoria,,"Candidate B, Night Service, relies on the late-turn pool. My data says that pool is thin.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Takumi,,Candidate B aligns with the successful pilot. The cost recovery was met.,"{""words"":12,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"Candidate D, Integrated Fare, requires data agreement. The committee minutes say that never happened.","{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Daisuke,,"Candidate D replaces ticketing. The fees are high, but the ledger shows the operating line is tight.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Sakura,,"Candidate A, Bus Corridor, addresses the mid-city route. But the timed runs show the issue is waiting.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Toshio, if D is stuck on data terms, it is a no-go for me.","{""words"":14,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Victoria, B is risky on staffing, but the pilot proves the concept.","{""words"":12,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Takumi, the pilots are eight years old. The current committee is different.","{""words"":12,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Daisuke,,"@Sakura, if the time is in waiting, A might not solve the core problem.","{""words"":14,""mentions"":[""Sakura""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Sakura,,"@Daisuke, yes. The journey audits point to connections, not surface routes.","{""words"":11,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Victoria,,"Candidate C, Station Access, uses the lift maintenance contract. That has spare capacity.","{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Takumi,,Candidate C fits the accessibility records. The lift rebuilds finished on time.,"{""words"":12,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"Candidate C involves pre-war shells. The contingency pricing is high, but the work is contained.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Daisuke,,"Candidate C is capital intensive. The ledger shows a ring-fenced balance, which might cover it.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Sakura,,"Candidate C improves station flow. The journey audits show waiting is the issue, and better access helps.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Victoria,,"I vote for C. The maintenance contract supports it, and it avoids the roster risks.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Takumi,,"I vote for C. The accessibility records show it is reliable, unlike the lane-priority work.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Toshio,,I vote for C. It avoids the data agreement trap of D and the staffing risk of B.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Daisuke,,"I vote for C. The capital balance can cover it, and it addresses the physical network.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Sakura,,"I vote for C. It improves the station experience, which is where the waiting happens.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
